item.text:
Reused evidence
None from the existing dossier maps cleanly to Chapter 4B, so I used a fresh 2022-targeted set.

New evidence
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"The government mobilized law-enforcement resources to bring civil and criminal charges against opposition leaders; the BNP said thousands of members were charged over demonstrations, and observers said many cases were politically motivated, including arrests on December 9 before the December 10 BNP rally.","locator":"Section 3, lines 279-285","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous government report with specific 2022 incidents and state attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 events.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and senior police/party actors rather than personal orders.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says civilian authorities retained formal control over security forces and the constitution still provides for periodic elections."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"The government restricted and disrupted internet access in numerous incidents, including blocking or slowing service in cities where opposition parties planned rallies, while also monitoring private online communications without proper legal authority.","locator":"Section 2.a, lines 181-186","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific 2022 examples in an official report with clear time and mechanism detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 events.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and telecom/security authorities; not a claim of personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes some privately owned newspapers still carried diverse views outside politically sensitive topics."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"Opposition BNP gatherings were regularly denied permission or confined to specific places, and police or ruling-party activists sometimes used force to disperse demonstrations; the government generally did not respect peaceful assembly.","locator":"Section 2.b, lines 196-203","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed 2022 reporting on repeated assembly restrictions and enforcement behavior.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 events.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the ruling government, police, and Awami League-aligned actors.","contrary_evidence":["The report acknowledges the law provides a formal right to peaceful assembly and that permission was sometimes granted under conditions."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"The government used transnational repression and related harassment to intimidate critics abroad and relatives at home, including surveillance, reprisals, and pressure on diaspora critics and their families.","locator":"Section 3, lines 133-144","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official report with concrete examples and direct attribution to state behavior.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 events.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and security/police services; not necessarily to Hasina personally.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that some critics pursued judicial remedies, though courts were often insufficiently independent."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"The report says refugees faced movement restrictions and that violence targeted ethnic minority, Indigenous, and LGBTQI+ people, while political participation remained seriously constrained for opposition-linked actors.","locator":"Executive summary, lines 38-39; Section 3, lines 268-270","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Broad but specific 2022 rights summary from a primary government report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level conditions under the Hasina government, not a single ordered event.","contrary_evidence":["The report is not a single-incident finding and does not isolate personal direction for every abuse."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Crackdown on Political Opposition","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/10/bangladesh-crackdown-political-opposition","claim":"HRW said opposition groups reported an escalation of repression, including mass arrests and police raids of opposition homes, while ruling-party supporters attacked opposition meetings and authorities failed to investigate them properly.","locator":"Body text, paragraphs 1-6","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established rights monitor with contemporaneous reporting, named incidents, and direct quotations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 events.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh authorities and ruling-party supporters under Hasina’s government.","contrary_evidence":["HRW notes each side accused the other of instigating violence, so some incident-level fault remains contested."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Two Bangladeshi opposition leaders arrested in government crackdown","publisher":"The Guardian","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/dec/09/bangladesh-opposition-leaders-arrested-crackdown-government-opponents","claim":"Two senior BNP leaders were arrested amid a crackdown in which at least seven people were shot dead and thousands arrested, with the BNP saying the detentions were meant to stop a planned Dhaka rally.","locator":"Lead paragraph and mid-article report on arrests and casualties","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Major independent outlet citing named parties, dates, and on-the-ground claims; still journalistic rather than adjudicative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Hasina’s government and police actions; not a finding of personal command.","contrary_evidence":["The government said arrests were on suspicion of instigating violence, so its justification was defensive rather than openly suppressive."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladeshi journalist Imran Hossain Titu investigated under Digital Security Act","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-08-23","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/08/bangladeshi-journalist-imran-hossain-titu-investigated-under-digital-security-act/","claim":"A Bangladeshi journalist was investigated under the Digital Security Act after a video report alleging corruption by a local shrine management committee tied to an administrative government unit.","locator":"Case description, paragraph on the April 5 complaint and March 1 broadcast","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist press-freedom monitor with specific documentary corroboration and dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows use of the law under the Hasina-era state, though the complaint was lodged through local legal channels.","contrary_evidence":["The case is one journalist and one complaint, so it does not by itself prove nationwide policy."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Digital security act: Cases filed mostly out of political motivation","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-02-17","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/digital-security-act-journos-general-citizens-mostly-targeted-2964651","claim":"Speakers said journalists and ordinary citizens were frequently targeted under the Digital Security Act for online speech, and CGS data suggested the cases were largely politically motivated.","locator":"Webinar report and CGS summary in the article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting based on a research presentation; not primary but specific and contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers early 2022 and summarizes up to January 2022 data.","ruler_attribution":"Targets the governing environment under Hasina rather than a single personal act.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports government defenders saying the law was needed for cybercrime and public order."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Digital Security Act hinders freedom of media: Editor’s Council","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/amp/story/bangladesh/digital-security-act-hinders-freedom-of-media-editors-council","claim":"Bangladesh’s Editors’ Council said the Digital Security Act obstructed media freedom and that journalists, activists, artists, and writers had been hit with cases under it; the article also reports the law minister acknowledged misuse.","locator":"Article body and quoted Editors’ Council statement","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local outlet reporting a public statement by editors, with direct government acknowledgment of abuse.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government’s legal environment and ministerial stance.","contrary_evidence":["The law minister said the government would not repeal the law and framed it as cybercrime enforcement, not repression."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Internet Impact Brief: Bangladesh: Regulation for Digital, Social Media and OTT Platforms, 2021","publisher":"Internet Society","publication_date":"2022-03-04","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/resources/doc/2022/internet-impact-brief-bangladesh-regulation-for-digital-social-media-and-ott-platforms-2021/","claim":"The draft BTRC regulation would require traceability of messages, 72-hour takedowns of flagged content, data retention, and broader intermediary duties that could weaken encryption and expand state control over online speech.","locator":"Context and numbered requirements, especially lines 73-93 and 117-124","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Technical policy analysis by a reputable internet-governance organization using the draft text directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 draft from Bangladesh regulators.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government’s telecom regulator and policy apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The brief notes some clauses purport to safeguard free speech and privacy, though it argues they are insufficient."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh 2022 - EN","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://rsf.org/en/analyse_regionale/663","claim":"RSF described Bangladesh’s state broadcasters as government propaganda outlets, said Awami League supporters often use targeted physical violence and judicial harassment against disliked ჟურნალისტs, and called the Digital Security Act one of the world’s most draconian laws for journalists.","locator":"Media landscape, political context, and legal framework sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist press-freedom monitor with country-level analysis, though not a legal finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Country analysis anchored in the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes the hostile media environment to Sheikh Hasina’s government and Awami League supporters.","contrary_evidence":["RSF also notes that Prothom Alo and The Daily Star retained some editorial independence, so the media space was not fully closed."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No scope to go back to caretaker regime: Law minister","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/elections/news/no-scope-go-back-caretaker-regime-law-minister-3131236","claim":"The law minister said there was no scope to restore the caretaker government system because the Supreme Court had declared it illegal, rejecting the opposition’s election-time neutrality demand.","locator":"Article body and quoted ministerial remarks","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct quote from a senior government official on a core transfer-rule issue.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government through its law minister, not to a separate institution.","contrary_evidence":["The statement invokes the court and constitution, but it also concretely blocks the opposition’s preferred election-time transfer mechanism."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.2","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"If someone doesn’t join election, it doesn’t mean there’s no democracy: PM Hasina","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/politics/wx1ccyiwqv","claim":"Hasina said a party’s refusal to join an election does not mean democracy is absent and added that if people do not want her, she does not want to remain in power.","locator":"Reported remarks from a Politico/NatSec Daily interview republished by Prothom Alo","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Directly reported ruler self-claim, but politically self-serving and contradicted by other evidence in the dossier.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Self-claim by Sheikh Hasina; useful for rhetoric, weak for actual conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Independent reporting in the same period describes arrests, censorship, and assembly restrictions that undercut the claim."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Awami League rallies: Police remain silent over permission, condition","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/amp/story/bangladesh/politics/tnyyt1ien8","claim":"Police were strict in denying BNP a road rally but allowed Awami League rallies with fewer constraints, and BNP was permitted to rally at Suhrawardy Udyan only on 26 conditions.","locator":"Article body describing rally permissions and contrasting treatment","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting on a specific administrative decision with clear comparative treatment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Dhaka police and the ruling-party environment under Hasina’s government.","contrary_evidence":["The article shows some opposition permission existed, but only under heavy conditions and venue restriction."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I screened roughly 30 documents across the State Department, HRW, Guardian, CPJ, RSF, Internet Society, Prothom Alo, The Daily Star, bdnews24, BSS, and a few Reuters/Al Jazeera derivatives. I kept the strongest non-duplicative claims and dropped repeated coverage of the same December 2022 crackdown, 2023-2024 retrospectives that were outside the target year, and structured Freedom House/RSF/Net pages whose underlying indicator content was already supplied in the local prior. I also treated a few government/party pages as context only when they were just self-justifying rhetoric rather than independently useful evidence.

Remaining gaps
The main gap is direct personal attribution. The record strongly supports a Hasina-government pattern of restriction, but there is little clean 2022 evidence that Sheikh Hasina herself ordered a specific arrest, content block, or police action. Institutional capture also remains better evidenced than formal court or legislature strengthening, so 4B.4 is thin. If you want a tighter dossier, the best next targets are election commission records, court rulings, and parliamentary proceedings from 2022, plus any contemporaneous official directives on protest permissions or digital-content blocking.

I did not score.
